Marvel's Midnight Suns is one of my all-time favorite games, so when I learned that many of the people who worked on that game were releasing a new tactics game, I immediately put it on top of my to-play list for 2026.  And Star Wars Zero Company delivered in spades, this is an incredibly fun game with incredible graphics, great characters and a good story, and that's coming from someone who's not a big Star Wars fan.

The core gameplay loop of Zero Company is very similar to Midnight Suns: you move around your home base, The Den, interacting with the other characters and then you select a mission to go out on, where the game becomes similar to a Star Wars Xcom variant.  Resources gathered on the missions are then funneled back into upgrading the Den and your team, and this makes for a very fun loop.

The actual missions themselves are varied, and although there are basically only a small handful of enemy types, they never felt too repetitive to me.  There's some fun new mechanics in Zero Company, such as the assist sytem where your characters can take a second free shot at an enemy, and at the same time level up their bonds, which means you're playing strategically with what pairs of characters should be assisting each other, and that was a lot of fun.  Turns use the typical three action points system common to other Xcom-like games, but also an advantage system which lets you do additional powerful actions based upon how many advantage points your team has built up.  In practice, this meant that each turn I was planning out which advantage actions I wanted to use, and figuring out the best path to build up enough advantage points to get there, which was a very fun twist on the Xcom formula.

I did find myself wishing for an "undo" button during the tactics sequences, for a few reasons.  Playing the game with a controller, sometimes the action I thought I was taking wasn't exactly the action that was registered with the game.  Also, it would be nice to be able to use some of your first moves to scout out what would be possible - so, move your character to a new location, and then see what they can realisitacally do from that location, then undo the move if you don't like that outcome.

Some of the main story missions include third person sections, where you're walking your crew between combat arenas, which is an interesting idea, but didn't add much to the game for me owing to how linear the world maps were and how little there was to do in them.

Unlike in Midnight Suns, you increase your bond with your teammates mostly through actions in combat rather than through interactions back at The Den.  This choice has a couple of practical consequences - it means that if you're not steadily rotating the composition of the squad you're sending into combat, you will not be able to max out all the relatonships.  That's an interesting forcing factor, although it does seem odd that your bond with your teammates grows when you give an assist in combat, but not when you're having a personal discussion with them about something going on in their lives.  Since character upgrades are directly tied to increasing the bond level between each pair of characters, I actually found myself sticking with my core group of characters for most of the game, and maxing out their bonds so that they would be as strong as possible.

While using resources gathered during missions to upgrade The Den was a compelling gameplay hook, I wish the system went a bit further.  The Den itself stayed relatively static, it would have been nice for it to be visually upgrading as the upgrades completed as well.  Compared to the Abby in Midnight Suns, the Den here is thankfully much smaller, making it a lot quicker to do the rounds and talk to all your teammates between missons.

The core cast of authored characters were engaging to interact with, and had some good character development as the game went along.  You also can recruit other characters to bring on missions with you, although I never really ever ended up using them, choosing instead to stay with the authored characters of the story.  In fact, I typically wound up doing all of my missions with Jae, Tel-Rea, and Cly; although I did very much enjoy interacting with Kabb back at The Den and did bring him along occasionally on missions, just because I found his in-combat quips entertaining "What's for supper? Cutty-upper!"  "Fist in gutty, upper cutty!"

I found the story the game was telling to be engaging, and it was often a driver of wanting to play one more cycle, despite me not being much of a Star Wars fan.  The team's attempt to figure out what The Coil and Fathom are was compelling, and the plot took a few twists that I didn't see coming.  I'm sure there was even more there if you are someone who's big into Star Wars, but even without that fandom it was enjoyable.

I went in with high hopes for Star Wars Zero Company based upon its pedigre, and it did not let me down.  This was a game I did not want to put down at the end of the night, and I could easily see this being one of my favorites of the year when all is said and done.
